{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_1>\nn = input()\n# Replace first occurrence of 'at' (not at position 0) with '@'\npos = 1\nwhile pos < len(n) - 1:\n    if n[pos:pos+2] == 'at':\n        n = n[:pos] + '@' + n[pos+2:]\n        break\n    pos += 1\n\n# Replace all occurrences of 'dot' (not at position 0) with '.'\npos = 1\nwhile pos < len(n) - 2:\n    if n[pos:pos+3] == 'dot':\n        n = n[:pos] + '.' + n[pos+3:]\n        pos -= 1  # Adjust position to check overlapping cases\n        if pos < 1:\n            pos = 1\n    else:\n        pos += 1\n\nprint(n)\n</your_rewritten_code_1>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nn = input()\n\n# We need to find the '@' symbol which was written as 'at'\n# and replace exactly one 'at' with '@' (not at start or end)\n# Then replace 'dot' with '.' (not at start or end)\n\n# First, find the position of 'at' that should become '@'\n# It should be somewhere in the middle (not at start or end)\nat_pos = -1\nfor i in range(1, len(n) - 1):\n    if n[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_pos = i\n        break\n\n# If we found 'at', replace it with '@'\nif at_pos != -1:\n    n = n[:at_pos] + '@' + n[at_pos+2:]\n\n# Now replace 'dot' with '.', but not at the beginning or end\n# We need to be careful about overlapping and multiple dots\nresult = []\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(n):\n    if i == 0 or i == len(n) - 1:\n        result.append(n[i])\n        i += 1\n    elif n[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n        # Check if this 'dot' is at the beginning or end of the string\n        # Actually, we need to check if replacing would make it start/end with '.'\n        # Since we're building the string, we check if current position is 0 or would be last\n        # But we're at position i, so if we replace, the '.' would be at position len(result)\n        # So we need to check if len(result) == 0 or len(result) + 1 == len(n) - (i - len(result)) ... this is complex\n        \n        # Simpler: we know the original string doesn't start/end with 'dot' as a word\n        # But after replacing 'at', we need to ensure we don't create a string starting/ending with '.'\n        # So we check if we're at the beginning of the result or if this would be the last character\n        if len(result) == 0 or (len(result) == len(n) - i - 1):  # This condition is not quite right\n        \n        # Let's use a different approach: replace 'dot' only if it's not at position 0 or len-3 in the current string\n        # But since we're building the string, let's track positions differently\n        \n        # Actually, let's reconsider: we should replace 'dot' only when it's not at the very beginning or very end\n        # of the entire string. But after replacing 'at', the string length changes.\n        \n        # Better approach: identify all 'dot' occurrences that are not at positions 0 or len-3 in the original string\n        # But after replacing 'at', positions shift.\n        \n        # Let's try a cleaner approach: \n        # 1. Find the '@' position (first valid 'at' not at start/end)\n        # 2. Then process the string to replace 'dot' with '.', but skip if it would be at start or end\n        \n        # Since the code is getting complex, let's rewrite more carefully\n        \n        # For now, let's just replace 'dot' if it's not at position 0 or len-3 in the current string\n        # But we need to be careful about the boundaries\n        if i > 0 and i + 3 < len(n):  # Not at start or end of current string\n            result.append('.')\n            i += 3\n        else:\n            result.append(n[i])\n            i += 1\n    else:\n        result.append(n[i])\n        i += 1\n\nn = ''.join(result)\n\nprint(n)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.00027855331427417696, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.8906041383743286, -1.311301275563892e-06, -9.894321920000948e-06, -0.5706659555435181, -6.67569793222355